# Standalone Aider (https://aider.chat), reachable directly over its own
# SSH server — same shape as this repo's own claude-cli environment, but
# provider-agnostic: point it at Anthropic directly, or at this repo's own
# llm-gateways LiteLLM proxy (Claude, DeepSeek, local Ollama models — pick
# per session), or at any other OpenAI-compatible endpoint. See README's
# "Choosing a Provider" before your first deploy — Aider needs an actual
# API key (billed usage), not a Claude.ai subscription login the way
# claude-cli's `/login` works.

# The container's name.
# Format: A single Docker container name.
CONTAINER_NAME=aider

# Host port that reaches this container's own sshd (mapped to its internal
# port 22). Distinct from claude-cli's own default (2222) so both can run
# on the same host without colliding.
# Format: A valid local TCP port.
SSH_PORT=2223

# Absolute path to the git repo (or any directory) you want `aider` to
# operate on. Bind-mounted to ~/workspace inside the container — that's
# where the auto-attached tmux session's `aider` starts.
# Format: An absolute path, or a leading ~ (expanded to your home directory
# when this is written to .env via deploy.sh's TUI — plain "~"/"~/..." only).
AIDER_WORKSPACE_PATH=~/aider-workspace

# Path to a file listing the public keys allowed to SSH into this
# container — one per line, standard authorized_keys format. Defaults to
# the same file your own host's sshd already trusts. Point it at a
# dedicated file instead if you want a narrower set of keys.
# Format: An absolute path, or a leading ~, to an existing authorized_keys
# file. Editing this file on the host takes effect on the container's next
# restart (FAST) — no rebuild needed.
SSH_AUTHORIZED_KEYS_PATH=~/.ssh/authorized_keys

# UID/GID the "aider" user inside the container is renumbered to at every
# start, so files Aider writes/commits in AIDER_WORKSPACE_PATH are owned by
# you on the host, not by an arbitrary container-only UID. Set to your own
# `id -u` / `id -g` if they're not the common default 1000/1000.
# Format: Integers.
PUID=1000
PGID=1000

# Git identity for commits Aider makes inside the container — unlike
# claude-cli, Aider commits automatically after each change by default
# (its own --auto-commits), so this matters more here. Commits fail
# without one configured.
# Format: Plain text / an email address.
#GIT_USER_NAME=Your Name
#GIT_USER_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Optional — a GitHub fine-grained personal access token
# (https://github.com/settings/tokens?type=beta), scoped to whichever
# repos you want reachable from inside this container. Only needed for
# non-interactive GitHub access over HTTPS without a live SSH agent
# forwarded in. Never written to a file under ~/.ssh — see entrypoint.sh's
# own comment for exactly where it ends up.
# Format: A GitHub personal access token string.
#GH_TOKEN=

# --- Provider configuration — see README's "Choosing a Provider" ---
# All three below are independent and optional; set whichever combination
# matches how you want to reach a model. Takes effect on the next restart
# (FAST is enough — no rebuild), same as CLAUDE_MODEL does in claude-cli.

# Direct Anthropic API — a real Anthropic Console API key
# (https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys), billed per token. This is
# NOT the same thing as the OAuth session claude-cli's `/login` creates;
# Aider is not Claude Code and doesn't use that flow.
# Format: An Anthropic API key string (sk-ant-...).
#ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Route through an OpenAI-compatible gateway instead — e.g. this repo's own
# llm-gateways environment's LiteLLM proxy, which can front Claude,
# DeepSeek, or local Ollama models behind one endpoint (see that
# environment's README and litellm-config.yaml for what's registered).
# llm-gateways and this environment are separate Compose projects with no
# shared Docker network, so OPENAI_API_BASE must reach it through the HOST
# itself (http://host.docker.internal:<LITELLM_PORT>/v1), not a container
# name — same reasoning claude-cli's own README gives for its gateway
# option. OPENAI_API_KEY is llm-gateways' own LITELLM_MASTER_KEY in that
# case. Leave both empty/unset to skip the gateway and use
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (or another provider's own env var — see README)
# directly instead.
# Format: A URL ending in /v1, and a bearer token/API key string.
#OPENAI_API_BASE=http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1
#OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Which model `aider` launches with, passed as `aider --model <this>`.
# Empty/unset lets Aider fall back to its own default, or prompt you to
# pick one if no provider key above is set at all. When routing through
# OPENAI_API_BASE above, prefix with "openai/" to force Aider's own
# litellm backend to use the OpenAI-compatible client (e.g.
# "openai/claude-sonnet" for a model_name registered that way in
# llm-gateways' litellm-config.yaml) — see README for why. Normally set via
# the "Choose Model" menu action rather than by hand.
# Format: A model name Aider/litellm recognizes — see README's "Choosing a
# Provider" for concrete examples against each provider above.
#AIDER_MODEL=

# --- Frontend options — see README's "Frontend Options" ---

# Frontend Option A (Aider's own built-in --gui, EXPERIMENTAL per Aider's
# own docs) — same container as the plain SSH/tmux service, launched
# on-demand via the "Launch Aider (Browser GUI)" menu action, not started
# automatically. Just a port reservation; nothing listens here until you
# actually launch it.
# Format: A valid local TCP port.
AIDER_GUI_PORT=8501

# Frontend Option B (OpenVSCode Server) — a full browser-based IDE with
# Aider pre-installed in its integrated terminal, sharing the same
# AIDER_WORKSPACE_PATH as the plain SSH/tmux service. Opt-in: add "ide" to
# COMPOSE_PROFILES below to actually build/start it — this variable alone
# does nothing.
# Format: A valid local TCP port.
AIDER_IDE_PORT=8443

# Which optional services actually start — a REAL Docker Compose mechanism
# (COMPOSE_PROFILES is read straight out of .env automatically, nothing
# this repo invented; see chat-frontends'/llm-gateways' own .env.example
# for the identical pattern). The plain SSH/tmux `aider` service above has
# no profile restriction, so it always starts regardless of this value —
# only Frontend Option B (OpenVSCode Server) is gated behind it. Leave
# blank to skip it entirely (Frontend Option A needs no profile — it's the
# same always-on container, just an on-demand tmux session inside it).
# Format: Comma-separated profile names — "ide" to enable OpenVSCode
# Server, or blank.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=

# Only read by the aider-ide service (COMPOSE_PROFILES="ide") — containers
# default to UTC with no timezone info of their own otherwise.
# Format: An IANA timezone name (e.g. America/New_York), or Etc/UTC.
TZ=Etc/UTC
